1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,162
Your friend says science is hard?

2
00:00:02,162 --> 00:00:03,211
Let's test that.

3
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:07,601
This is the periodic table.

4
00:00:07,601 --> 00:00:08,998
118 elements.

5
00:00:08,998 --> 00:00:10,073
118 boxes.

6
00:00:10,073 --> 00:00:12,114
Looks scary, right?

7
00:00:12,433 --> 00:00:16,955
But by the end of this video, you will be able to point at any box and tell its

8
00:00:16,955 --> 00:00:17,298
story.

9
00:00:17,298 --> 00:00:18,156
And the secret?

10
00:00:18,156 --> 00:00:20,503
You already learned it in fifth standard.

11
00:00:21,433 --> 00:00:22,198
Chapter 2.

12
00:00:22,198 --> 00:00:24,949
Periodic Classification of Elements.

13
00:00:24,949 --> 00:00:25,637
Let's go!

14
00:00:28,199 --> 00:00:30,341
Every atom is like a tiny building.

15
00:00:30,341 --> 00:00:33,952
In the centre is the nucleus, packed with positive protons.

16
00:00:34,799 --> 00:00:38,515
<font color="#c084fc">Why don't the electrons just fly away from the nucleus?</font>

17
00:00:38,833 --> 00:00:39,759
Good question!

18
00:00:39,759 --> 00:00:43,001
Protons are positive, and electrons are negative.

19
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:44,457
And opposites attract.

20
00:00:44,457 --> 00:00:49,287
The nucleus keeps pulling the electrons, like a magnet holding iron pins.

21
00:00:49,287 --> 00:00:50,545
Remember this pull.

22
00:00:50,545 --> 00:00:53,655
It explains almost every trend in this chapter.

23
00:00:54,999 --> 00:00:58,549
Count the protons, and you get the atomic number, Z.

24
00:00:58,549 --> 00:01:01,348
Think of it as the element's roll number.

25
00:01:01,348 --> 00:01:05,308
Hydrogen is roll number 1, helium 2, lithium 3, and so on.

26
00:01:05,308 --> 00:01:07,766
No two elements share a roll number.

27
00:01:08,699 --> 00:01:14,491
Around the nucleus, electrons live on floors called shells: K, L, M and N.

28
00:01:14,491 --> 00:01:18,014
Each floor has a seat limit: 2, 8, 18 and 32.

29
00:01:18,014 --> 00:01:19,892
That's just 2 n squared.

30
00:01:20,233 --> 00:01:21,627
Let's fill sodium.

31
00:01:21,627 --> 00:01:25,191
Its roll number is 11, so it has 11 electrons.

32
00:01:25,191 --> 00:01:26,818
2 on the first floor.

33
00:01:26,818 --> 00:01:28,058
8 on the second.

34
00:01:28,058 --> 00:01:30,769
And 1 lonely electron on the third.

35
00:01:30,769 --> 00:01:32,164
Sodium is 2, 8, 1.

36
00:01:32,499 --> 00:01:35,643
That top floor electron is called the valence electron.

37
00:01:35,643 --> 00:01:39,072
It is the one at the window, the one that meets other atoms.

38
00:01:39,072 --> 00:01:41,300
And it decides how the element behaves.

39
00:01:42,933 --> 00:01:46,161
Sodium has just one, and it would happily give it away.

40
00:01:46,161 --> 00:01:48,802
Elements that give away electrons are metals.

41
00:01:49,133 --> 00:01:50,559
Chlorine is 2, 8, 7.

42
00:01:50,559 --> 00:01:54,981
It needs just one more to fill its top floor, so it grabs one.

43
00:01:54,981 --> 00:01:58,120
Elements that grab electrons are non-metals.

44
00:01:58,466 --> 00:02:01,549
And argon, 2, 8, 8, has a full top floor.

45
00:02:01,549 --> 00:02:02,601
Totally happy.

46
00:02:02,601 --> 00:02:04,481
It does not react at all.

47
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:08,350
<font color="#c084fc">Why does sodium give away 1 electron?</font>

48
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:10,633
<font color="#c084fc">Why not grab 7 more instead?</font>

49
00:02:10,966 --> 00:02:11,878
Think about it.

50
00:02:11,878 --> 00:02:15,769
Getting rid of 1 electron is much easier than collecting 7 more.

51
00:02:15,769 --> 00:02:19,903
Chlorine is the opposite: grabbing 1 is easier than throwing away 7.

52
00:02:19,903 --> 00:02:23,125
Atoms always take the easy way to a full outer shell.

53
00:02:23,125 --> 00:02:27,442
That's why metals have few valence electrons, and non-metals have many.

54
00:02:29,366 --> 00:02:33,624
So remember three things: roll number, floors, and top floor electrons.

55
00:02:33,624 --> 00:02:38,602
Honestly, the rest of this chapter is just these three, wearing different costumes.

56
00:02:39,533 --> 00:02:41,497
Now, a puzzle that took 100 years.

57
00:02:41,497 --> 00:02:46,352
In the eighteen hundreds, scientists kept discovering new elements, but there was no

58
00:02:46,352 --> 00:02:46,698
order.

59
00:02:46,698 --> 00:02:49,646
Like a cupboard where everything is just thrown in.

60
00:02:50,566 --> 00:02:52,724
Four scientists tried to bring order.

61
00:02:52,724 --> 00:02:54,357
Let's meet them, one by one.

62
00:02:55,199 --> 00:02:57,122
First, Dobereiner, in 1817.

63
00:02:57,122 --> 00:03:01,823
He found groups of three similar elements, and called them triads.

64
00:03:01,823 --> 00:03:04,743
And they had a magic trick hidden inside.

65
00:03:05,066 --> 00:03:07,954
Take lithium, 7, and potassium, 39.

66
00:03:07,954 --> 00:03:09,026
Add them: 46.

67
00:03:09,026 --> 00:03:10,429
Half of that: 23.

68
00:03:10,429 --> 00:03:13,647
And sodium's atomic mass is exactly 23!

69
00:03:13,647 --> 00:03:17,525
The middle one is the average of the other two.

70
00:03:17,866 --> 00:03:20,760
It works for calcium, strontium and barium.

71
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:23,519
And for chlorine, bromine and iodine too.

72
00:03:23,519 --> 00:03:25,942
But he could find only a few triads.

73
00:03:25,942 --> 00:03:27,691
Most elements had no team.

74
00:03:27,691 --> 00:03:30,720
So this idea could not classify all elements.

75
00:03:31,566 --> 00:03:34,294
<font color="#c084fc">Why did they arrange elements by mass?</font>

76
00:03:34,294 --> 00:03:36,088
<font color="#c084fc">Why not by atomic number?</font>

77
00:03:36,433 --> 00:03:41,184
Because back then, nobody knew about protons or electrons inside the atom!

78
00:03:41,184 --> 00:03:45,357
The only thing scientists could measure was how heavy an atom is.

79
00:03:45,357 --> 00:03:48,053
So atomic mass was the best tool they had.

80
00:03:48,053 --> 00:03:50,557
Atomic number came much later, in 1913.

81
00:03:52,499 --> 00:03:54,279
Next, Newlands, in 1866.

82
00:03:54,279 --> 00:04:00,509
He lined up the elements in increasing order of atomic mass, starting from hydrogen,

83
00:04:00,509 --> 00:04:03,698
all the way to thorium, 56 elements in all.

84
00:04:04,033 --> 00:04:06,574
And he noticed something musical.

85
00:04:06,574 --> 00:04:09,961
Every eighth element behaves like the first.

86
00:04:09,961 --> 00:04:13,580
Sa, re, ga, ma, pa, dha, ni, and then sa again!

87
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:16,044
He called it the law of octaves.

88
00:04:16,366 --> 00:04:20,367
Look: lithium, sodium and potassium land in the same column.

89
00:04:20,367 --> 00:04:22,301
All three are similar metals.

90
00:04:22,301 --> 00:04:24,901
Fluorine and chlorine are together too.

91
00:04:24,901 --> 00:04:25,568
Beautiful!

92
00:04:25,899 --> 00:04:28,361
But after calcium, the music went off-key.

93
00:04:28,361 --> 00:04:32,933
He squeezed cobalt and nickel into one box, and put them in the same column as

94
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:34,223
fluorine and chlorine.

95
00:04:34,223 --> 00:04:36,802
That's like putting a cat in a team of fish!

96
00:04:36,802 --> 00:04:39,792
And he assumed no new elements would ever be found.

97
00:04:40,633 --> 00:04:43,140
<font color="#c084fc">Why did the pattern break after calcium?</font>

98
00:04:43,466 --> 00:04:48,512
Because after calcium, ten new elements squeeze in between: the transition metals,

99
00:04:48,512 --> 00:04:50,789
like iron, cobalt, nickel and copper.

100
00:04:50,789 --> 00:04:53,927
So from there, the pattern repeats after 18, not 8.

101
00:04:53,927 --> 00:04:57,127
Newlands forced everything into eights, so it broke.

102
00:04:57,127 --> 00:04:59,773
The modern table fixes this, as you'll see.

103
00:05:01,699 --> 00:05:04,189
Then came Dmitri Mendeleev, in 1869.

104
00:05:04,189 --> 00:05:06,334
63 elements were known by then.

105
00:05:06,334 --> 00:05:11,867
He wrote each one on a card with its properties, and kept arranging them, like a

106
00:05:11,867 --> 00:05:13,043
game of patience.

107
00:05:13,366 --> 00:05:17,771
He arranged them by atomic mass, and also checked their chemical properties,

108
00:05:17,771 --> 00:05:20,842
especially the formulas of their oxides and hydrides.

109
00:05:20,842 --> 00:05:23,856
That means their compounds with oxygen and hydrogen.

110
00:05:24,699 --> 00:05:30,123
His law says: properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic masses.

111
00:05:30,123 --> 00:05:33,351
Periodic means, repeating after regular intervals.

112
00:05:33,351 --> 00:05:35,869
Learn this line exactly, word for word.

113
00:05:36,699 --> 00:05:38,190
Now, his master stroke.

114
00:05:38,190 --> 00:05:41,755
Where no known element fitted, he did not force one in.

115
00:05:41,755 --> 00:05:46,487
He left an empty chair, and said: someone will come and sit here one day.

116
00:05:46,833 --> 00:05:51,963
He even predicted their properties, and gave them names: eka-boron, eka-aluminium

117
00:05:51,963 --> 00:05:52,977
and eka-silicon.

118
00:05:52,977 --> 00:05:54,687
Eka means one, in Sanskrit.

119
00:05:55,933 --> 00:05:59,404
<font color="#c084fc">How could he predict an element that nobody had ever seen?</font>

120
00:05:59,733 --> 00:06:01,888
By looking at its neighbours!

121
00:06:01,888 --> 00:06:05,232
In the table, properties change step by step.

122
00:06:05,232 --> 00:06:08,725
Aluminium is above the gap, indium is below it.

123
00:06:08,725 --> 00:06:12,738
So the missing element should be somewhere in between.

124
00:06:12,738 --> 00:06:15,116
The average of 27 and 115 is 71.

125
00:06:15,116 --> 00:06:16,825
The real gallium: 69.7.

126
00:06:16,825 --> 00:06:17,494
So close!

127
00:06:17,494 --> 00:06:21,359
That's Dobereiner's averaging idea, used like a pro.

128
00:06:22,699 --> 00:06:25,914
Years later, gallium was discovered.

129
00:06:25,914 --> 00:06:29,754
Compare his prediction with the real thing.

130
00:06:29,754 --> 00:06:32,076
Atomic mass 68, real 69.7.

131
00:06:32,076 --> 00:06:34,129
Density 5.9, real 5.94.

132
00:06:34,129 --> 00:06:36,898
Oxide formula E2O3, real Ga2O3.

133
00:06:36,898 --> 00:06:38,058
He nailed it!

134
00:06:38,058 --> 00:06:42,613
Scandium and germanium matched his predictions too.

135
00:06:43,533 --> 00:06:46,489
He also corrected some wrong atomic masses.

136
00:06:46,489 --> 00:06:49,376
Beryllium was corrected from 14.09 to 9.4.

137
00:06:49,376 --> 00:06:55,083
And when noble gases like helium, neon and argon were discovered later, they simply

138
00:06:55,083 --> 00:06:56,939
joined as a new zero group.

139
00:06:56,939 --> 00:06:58,795
Nothing in the table broke.

140
00:06:59,733 --> 00:07:01,592
But his table had a few cracks.

141
00:07:01,592 --> 00:07:02,972
Where does hydrogen go?

142
00:07:02,972 --> 00:07:07,471
In some ways it behaves like the alkali metals, and in other ways, like the

143
00:07:07,471 --> 00:07:08,011
halogens.

144
00:07:08,333 --> 00:07:11,317
Isotopes, like chlorine 35 and chlorine 37.

145
00:07:11,317 --> 00:07:14,232
Same element, but different atomic masses.

146
00:07:14,232 --> 00:07:17,703
In a table based on mass, which seat do they take?

147
00:07:18,633 --> 00:07:23,187
And cobalt, with atomic mass 58.9, was placed before nickel, 58.7.

148
00:07:23,187 --> 00:07:24,774
Heavier before lighter.

149
00:07:24,774 --> 00:07:26,499
That breaks his own rule!

150
00:07:26,499 --> 00:07:32,088
Also, atomic masses don't increase in a regular way, so nobody could say how many

151
00:07:32,088 --> 00:07:34,986
elements might exist between two elements.

152
00:07:35,833 --> 00:07:40,024
<font color="#c084fc">If his rule was atomic mass, why did he put cobalt first?</font>

153
00:07:40,366 --> 00:07:43,096
Because he trusted properties more than mass.

154
00:07:43,096 --> 00:07:47,827
Cobalt behaves like rhodium and iridium, and nickel behaves like palladium and

155
00:07:47,827 --> 00:07:48,373
platinum.

156
00:07:48,373 --> 00:07:50,617
So he placed them by their behaviour.

157
00:07:50,617 --> 00:07:51,405
He was right!

158
00:07:51,405 --> 00:07:53,164
He just couldn't explain why.

159
00:07:53,164 --> 00:07:55,409
That answer came later, from Moseley.

160
00:07:57,333 --> 00:08:03,316
In 1913, a young English scientist, Henry Moseley, fixed all of this with one simple

161
00:08:03,316 --> 00:08:03,672
idea.

162
00:08:04,399 --> 00:08:07,935
Imagine your teacher arranging the class by weight.

163
00:08:07,935 --> 00:08:10,222
Two students weigh 42 kilos each.

164
00:08:10,222 --> 00:08:11,332
Who comes first?

165
00:08:11,332 --> 00:08:12,510
Confusing, right?

166
00:08:12,833 --> 00:08:14,865
Now arrange by roll number.

167
00:08:14,865 --> 00:08:15,693
1, 2, 3, 4.

168
00:08:15,693 --> 00:08:17,952
Always clear, never confusing.

169
00:08:17,952 --> 00:08:21,791
For elements, the roll number is the atomic number.

170
00:08:22,633 --> 00:08:25,995
Cobalt's roll number is 27, nickel's is 28.

171
00:08:25,995 --> 00:08:28,576
So cobalt comes first, naturally.

172
00:08:28,576 --> 00:08:29,749
No rule broken.

173
00:08:29,749 --> 00:08:30,766
And isotopes?

174
00:08:30,766 --> 00:08:34,911
Chlorine 35 and chlorine 37 both have roll number 17.

175
00:08:34,911 --> 00:08:37,100
So they share the same seat.

176
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:38,273
Problem solved!

177
00:08:39,599 --> 00:08:42,687
<font color="#c084fc">But why is atomic number better than mass?</font>

178
00:08:42,687 --> 00:08:45,039
<font color="#c084fc">They both go up together anyway.</font>

179
00:08:45,366 --> 00:08:46,821
Here's the deep reason.

180
00:08:46,821 --> 00:08:48,782
Chemistry is done by electrons.

181
00:08:48,782 --> 00:08:53,591
And the number of electrons equals the number of protons, the atomic number.

182
00:08:53,591 --> 00:08:55,046
Neutrons only add mass.

183
00:08:55,046 --> 00:08:57,513
They don't change the electrons at all.

184
00:08:57,513 --> 00:09:02,638
So mass carries extra noise from neutrons, but atomic number tells exactly how an

185
00:09:02,638 --> 00:09:03,903
element will behave.

186
00:09:05,233 --> 00:09:07,476
So the law changed by just one word.

187
00:09:07,476 --> 00:09:11,900
Properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic numbers.

188
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:13,084
Mass became number.

189
00:09:13,084 --> 00:09:15,015
That's the modern periodic law.

190
00:09:16,433 --> 00:09:21,722
Now watch the modern periodic table build itself, using only our three basics.

191
00:09:21,722 --> 00:09:24,299
Hydrogen has 1 electron, helium has 2.

192
00:09:24,299 --> 00:09:27,486
Both use just one floor, so they make period 1.

193
00:09:27,833 --> 00:09:30,019
Lithium to neon need two floors.

194
00:09:30,019 --> 00:09:31,113
That's period 2.

195
00:09:31,113 --> 00:09:34,120
Sodium to argon need three floors: period 3.

196
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:37,673
So the period number is simply the number of floors!

197
00:09:37,999 --> 00:09:39,929
Now look down a column.

198
00:09:39,929 --> 00:09:41,019
Lithium 2, 1.

199
00:09:41,019 --> 00:09:42,278
Sodium 2, 8, 1.

200
00:09:42,278 --> 00:09:44,039
Potassium 2, 8, 8, 1.

201
00:09:44,039 --> 00:09:46,724
All have one top-floor electron.

202
00:09:46,724 --> 00:09:51,254
Same valence electrons, same group, similar behaviour.

203
00:09:52,099 --> 00:09:52,661
<font color="#c084fc">Wait!</font>

204
00:09:52,661 --> 00:09:55,356
<font color="#c084fc">The M shell can hold 18.</font>

205
00:09:55,356 --> 00:10:00,633
<font color="#c084fc">So why is potassium 2, 8, 8, 1 and not 2, 8, 9?</font>

206
00:10:00,966 --> 00:10:02,010
Brilliant doubt!

207
00:10:02,010 --> 00:10:07,037
There's one more rule: the outermost shell cannot hold more than 8 electrons.

208
00:10:07,037 --> 00:10:11,541
So once the M shell has 8, the next electron moves up to the N shell.

209
00:10:11,541 --> 00:10:14,870
That's why potassium starts a new period: period 4.

210
00:10:16,199 --> 00:10:18,754
Quick trick, for chlorine, 2, 8, 7.

211
00:10:18,754 --> 00:10:20,652
Three floors, so period 3.

212
00:10:20,652 --> 00:10:24,228
Seven valence electrons, plus 10, gives group 17.

213
00:10:24,228 --> 00:10:29,848
For groups 1 and 2, the group number is just the number of valence electrons.

214
00:10:30,666 --> 00:10:33,406
<font color="#c084fc">Helium has 2 valence electrons.</font>

215
00:10:33,406 --> 00:10:36,500
<font color="#c084fc">Why is it in group 18, not group 2?</font>

216
00:10:36,833 --> 00:10:40,014
Because helium's only shell, K, can hold just 2.

217
00:10:40,014 --> 00:10:41,406
So it's already full.

218
00:10:41,406 --> 00:10:46,045
All group 18 elements have a full outer shell, and none of them react.

219
00:10:46,045 --> 00:10:50,022
Helium behaves exactly like them, so it belongs in group 18.

220
00:10:50,022 --> 00:10:52,275
Groups are really about behaviour.

221
00:10:52,999 --> 00:10:53,831
And hydrogen?

222
00:10:53,831 --> 00:10:57,219
It has 1 valence electron, so it's placed in group 1.

223
00:10:57,219 --> 00:10:59,649
But it's a non-metal gas, not a metal.

224
00:10:59,649 --> 00:11:03,165
That's why hydrogen's position is still a special case.

225
00:11:04,999 --> 00:11:09,632
Keep going, and you get the full table: 7 periods, and 18 groups.

226
00:11:09,966 --> 00:11:11,194
It has four blocks.

227
00:11:11,194 --> 00:11:13,198
Groups 1 and 2 are the s-block.

228
00:11:13,198 --> 00:11:15,267
Groups 13 to 18 are the p-block.

229
00:11:15,267 --> 00:11:20,050
And groups 3 to 12 in the middle are the d-block, the transition elements.

230
00:11:20,899 --> 00:11:24,406
<font color="#c084fc">Why does the table suddenly get wider from period 4?</font>

231
00:11:24,733 --> 00:11:29,675
From period 4, ten extra elements, the d-block, fit in between group 2 and group 13.

232
00:11:29,675 --> 00:11:32,440
That's the same reason Newlands' octaves broke!

233
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:37,029
These elements put their new electrons into an inner shell, not the outer one.

234
00:11:37,029 --> 00:11:41,972
So their outer shells look alike, and that's why transition metals are so similar to

235
00:11:41,972 --> 00:11:42,619
each other.

236
00:11:43,966 --> 00:11:48,515
The f-block, the lanthanides and actinides, is pulled out and kept at the bottom,

237
00:11:48,515 --> 00:11:50,874
just so the table doesn't become too wide.

238
00:11:51,199 --> 00:11:56,223
And this zig-zag line separates metals on the left from non-metals on the right.

239
00:11:56,223 --> 00:11:58,735
The metalloids sit right along the line.

240
00:11:58,735 --> 00:11:59,238
And see?

241
00:11:59,238 --> 00:12:00,808
Most elements are metals.

242
00:12:01,133 --> 00:12:07,901
And the number of elements in each period: 2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32, 32.

243
00:12:09,333 --> 00:12:10,320
Now the trends.

244
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:15,522
Many students try to memorise lots of arrows, and then forget them in the exam.

245
00:12:15,522 --> 00:12:15,917
Don't.

246
00:12:15,917 --> 00:12:17,629
You only need two reasons.

247
00:12:18,466 --> 00:12:19,291
Reason one.

248
00:12:19,291 --> 00:12:25,068
Across a period, the floors stay the same, but the nucleus gets more and more

249
00:12:25,068 --> 00:12:25,668
protons.

250
00:12:25,668 --> 00:12:30,770
So it pulls harder, the electrons come closer, and the atom shrinks.

251
00:12:30,770 --> 00:12:32,721
Lithium is 152 picometres.

252
00:12:32,721 --> 00:12:34,071
Oxygen is only 66.

253
00:12:34,899 --> 00:12:36,873
<font color="#c084fc">More protons AND more electrons...</font>

254
00:12:36,873 --> 00:12:38,615
<font color="#c084fc">shouldn't the atom get BIGGER?</font>

255
00:12:38,933 --> 00:12:40,511
It feels like it, right?

256
00:12:40,511 --> 00:12:43,340
But the new electrons go to the same floor.

257
00:12:43,340 --> 00:12:44,787
No new floor is added.

258
00:12:44,787 --> 00:12:50,181
Meanwhile, the nucleus gets stronger with every proton, and pulls that whole floor

259
00:12:50,181 --> 00:12:50,641
inward.

260
00:12:50,641 --> 00:12:53,272
Same floor, stronger pull: smaller atom.

261
00:12:55,099 --> 00:12:56,001
Reason two.

262
00:12:56,001 --> 00:13:01,740
Down a group, a new floor is added each time, so the atom gets bigger.

263
00:13:01,740 --> 00:13:05,838
Lithium 152, sodium 186, potassium 231 picometres.

264
00:13:05,838 --> 00:13:10,511
And one picometre is ten to the power minus twelve metre.

265
00:13:10,511 --> 00:13:11,413
Super tiny!

266
00:13:12,233 --> 00:13:15,003
<font color="#c084fc">But protons increase down a group too!</font>

267
00:13:15,003 --> 00:13:17,336
<font color="#c084fc">So why does the atom grow there?</font>

268
00:13:17,666 --> 00:13:19,317
Because the new floor wins.

269
00:13:19,317 --> 00:13:21,579
Each new floor sits much farther out.

270
00:13:21,579 --> 00:13:26,592
And the inner floors of electrons stand in between, blocking part of the nucleus's

271
00:13:26,592 --> 00:13:29,343
pull, like a crowd between you and the stage.

272
00:13:29,343 --> 00:13:33,683
So the outer electron feels only a weak pull, and the atom gets bigger.

273
00:13:35,533 --> 00:13:37,107
Now, metallic character.

274
00:13:37,107 --> 00:13:40,847
It simply means: how easily an atom gives away electrons.

275
00:13:40,847 --> 00:13:45,505
Across a period, the nucleus holds tighter, so giving away gets harder.

276
00:13:45,505 --> 00:13:47,408
Metallic character decreases.

277
00:13:47,408 --> 00:13:52,919
Sodium and magnesium are metals, silicon is a metalloid, and phosphorus, sulphur and

278
00:13:52,919 --> 00:13:54,493
chlorine are non-metals.

279
00:13:54,833 --> 00:13:59,938
Down a group, the top floor electrons are far from the nucleus, and loosely held.

280
00:13:59,938 --> 00:14:01,072
Easy to give away.

281
00:14:01,072 --> 00:14:03,089
So metallic character increases.

282
00:14:03,089 --> 00:14:06,241
That's why potassium is more reactive than sodium.

283
00:14:06,566 --> 00:14:09,368
Non-metallic character is the exact opposite.

284
00:14:09,368 --> 00:14:12,918
It increases across a period, and decreases down a group.

285
00:14:12,918 --> 00:14:16,842
Among the halogens, fluorine is the strongest electron grabber.

286
00:14:17,666 --> 00:14:20,383
<font color="#c084fc">Why is fluorine the best electron grabber?</font>

287
00:14:20,733 --> 00:14:23,606
Because fluorine is the smallest in its group.

288
00:14:23,606 --> 00:14:28,854
Its outer floor is very close to the nucleus, so an incoming electron feels a strong

289
00:14:28,854 --> 00:14:29,166
pull.

290
00:14:29,166 --> 00:14:30,665
Small atom, strong grip.

291
00:14:30,665 --> 00:14:32,976
Iodine is big, so its grip is weaker.

292
00:14:34,899 --> 00:14:38,498
Valency comes from the valence electrons.

293
00:14:38,498 --> 00:14:42,798
Across period 3: 1, 2, 3, 4, then 3, 2, 1, and 0.

294
00:14:42,798 --> 00:14:45,168
Up to four, then back down.

295
00:14:45,168 --> 00:14:48,327
And down a group, it stays the same.

296
00:14:49,166 --> 00:14:52,103
<font color="#c084fc">Why does valency go back DOWN after 4?</font>

297
00:14:52,433 --> 00:14:54,120
Remember the easy way?

298
00:14:54,120 --> 00:14:56,805
Phosphorus has 5 valence electrons.

299
00:14:56,805 --> 00:14:59,720
Losing 5 is hard, gaining 3 is easier.

300
00:14:59,720 --> 00:15:01,254
So its valency is 3.

301
00:15:01,254 --> 00:15:03,862
Sulphur needs 2, chlorine needs 1.

302
00:15:03,862 --> 00:15:06,546
And argon is already full, so zero.

303
00:15:08,466 --> 00:15:10,593
Here is every trend on one screen.

304
00:15:10,593 --> 00:15:15,536
And every single arrow comes from those two reasons: more protons across, a new

305
00:15:15,536 --> 00:15:16,225
floor down.

306
00:15:16,225 --> 00:15:18,602
Pause the video and take a screenshot.

307
00:15:22,033 --> 00:15:22,780
Quick test!

308
00:15:22,780 --> 00:15:23,867
Three questions.

309
00:15:23,867 --> 00:15:26,585
Try to answer before the timer runs out.

310
00:15:26,933 --> 00:15:28,610
An element is 2, 8, 2.

311
00:15:28,610 --> 00:15:30,821
Which period and which group?

312
00:15:30,821 --> 00:15:32,193
And is it a metal?

313
00:15:38,233 --> 00:15:40,252
Three floors, so period 3.

314
00:15:40,252 --> 00:15:42,894
Two valence electrons, so group 2.

315
00:15:42,894 --> 00:15:44,758
It's magnesium, a metal.

316
00:15:46,399 --> 00:15:48,662
Who left empty spaces in his table?

317
00:15:48,662 --> 00:15:51,247
And what was eka-aluminium later called?

318
00:15:57,266 --> 00:15:57,970
Mendeleev.

319
00:15:57,970 --> 00:16:00,012
And eka-aluminium is gallium.

320
00:16:01,633 --> 00:16:04,622
Which atom is bigger, sodium or potassium?

321
00:16:04,622 --> 00:16:05,192
And why?

322
00:16:11,233 --> 00:16:11,847
Potassium.

323
00:16:11,847 --> 00:16:14,490
Going down the group, a new floor is added.

324
00:16:16,733 --> 00:16:18,002
So, was it hard?

325
00:16:18,002 --> 00:16:19,033
Three basics.

326
00:16:19,033 --> 00:16:19,826
One story.

327
00:16:19,826 --> 00:16:20,778
Two reasons.

328
00:16:20,778 --> 00:16:22,841
That is the whole chapter.

329
00:16:23,166 --> 00:16:23,929
Myth busted!

330
00:16:23,929 --> 00:16:27,679
Now send this video to the friend who said science is hard.

331
00:16:27,679 --> 00:16:29,459
All the best for your exams!
